From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pokeplatinum-3ds <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 15:40:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] A building's sun shadow on higher ground in pieces, one
 shadow down lower ground; no sun shadows under a roof (SDK_PORT_3DS)

A building's shadow was one flat outline at the height it stands at, and
a whole copy for up to two higher heights, each drawn wherever nothing
stood in front of it. Past a dish's plaza, on the stepped wall down from
it, both showed side by side, the one laid in its pit and the one on the
plaza, each hanging over the wall at its own height: its shadow in
separate pieces.

Now the ground under the outline is looked up as a grid of the map's
tiles (the field's own heights; a tile not known yet is the building's
own ground, and the grid is looked at again every 90 frames until all
are known). Up to 6 heights, the building's own and the higher ones
found most often within 64 of it; for each the shadow of what stands
above that height (NitroPort_Gx3dShadowHullPartsFrom), cut to the tiles
at that height (NitroPort_Gx3dShadowPieces). Lower ground has no shadow
of its own: a tile lower than the ground its shadow comes over (the tile
before it up the light's way) takes that tile's, so the shadow runs on
down a wall as before, but one only. Kept as two display lists (the
shade, its soft edge) for the building's place until the sun moves on,
one building's made a frame. With no higher ground under it the outline
is drawn whole, as before.

Under a roof (the area's lighting not the outdoors') the port is told
there is no sun (NitroPort_SetSunRoofed): nothing is marked, everything is
drawn as the DS has it. A gate's planters cast the sun's wedges across
its floor.
